        <p>Australians spend a lot of money on sleep trackers. The global wearables market is worth billions, and a big chunk of that is people buying rings, watches, and bands hoping to finally understand why they wake up exhausted.</p>

        <p>But here's the honest truth: not all sleep trackers are created equal. Some give you genuinely useful data. Others give you numbers that look impressive but don't actually help you sleep better.</p>

        <p>We spent weeks testing the major sleep trackers available in Australia — wearing them, comparing the data, and Cross-referencing against clinical sleep studies where we could. Here's what we found.</p>

        <h2>What Actually Matters in a Sleep Tracker</h2>

        <p>Before diving into specific devices, it's worth knowing what sleep metrics are actually backed by research — and which are marketing fluff.</p>

        <p><strong>Sleep duration</strong> is the most straightforward. Every tracker measures it, and they're all roughly accurate for this. Most people know when they've slept 5 versus 8 hours.</p>

        <p><strong>Sleep stages</strong> (light, deep, REM) are where things get more interesting. Research-grade polysomnography (the kind used in sleep clinics) is the gold standard — and consumer wearables aren't there yet. Studies suggest most devices accurately detect deep sleep around 60-70% of the time. That's useful, but don't treat the numbers as gospel.</p>

        <p><strong>HRV (Heart Rate Variability)</strong> during sleep is emerging as one of the more useful metrics. Lower HRV during deep sleep can indicate physical stress; higher HRV generally correlates with recovery and nervous system resilience. Devices like Oura and Whoop have made this a centrepiece of their analytics.</p>

        <p><strong>Resting heart rate</strong> is another reliable metric. Elevated resting heart rate over multiple nights can signal overtraining, illness, or poor recovery.</p>

        <p>What to take with a grain of salt: "sleep scores," AI-curated recommendations, and any device that claims to detect specific dreams or diagnose conditions.</p>

